  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Friday)\nIssued at 1219 PM CDT Sat Oct 17 2026\n\nA beautiful, fall-like day is progressing across the Mid-South\nwith temperatures currently spanning the 50s into the low 60s.\nWinds will remain slightly elevated through the daytime hours as\nan upper-level trough continues to move eastward with a tight\npressure gradient lingering through the region. Wind gusts up to\n25 mph can be expected into the afternoon, with wind speeds\nfinally beginning to subside after sunset and into the overnight\nhours. With generally clear skies overnight and decreasing wind\nspeeds, we will see some decent radiational cooling. By early\nSunday morning, locations generally north of the I-40 corridor\nwill see low temperatures in the upper 30s with lows in the low\n40s elsewhere. Current HREF guidance has about a 30 to 50% chance\nof lows north of I-40 falling below 37 degrees. However, given\nthe uncertainty with our wind speeds overnight, opted against any\nFrost Headlines for our northern tier of counties that could fall\nbelow this threshold. While we will not rule out some isolated,\npatchy frost along the far northern tier of counties or low-lying\nregions, wind speeds may remain high enough to hinder widespread\nfrost development and temperatures will likely only fall that low\nfor an hour or two. For additional context, this is around the\ntime of year that we typically see our first frost for these\nlocations.\n\nAfter the chilly start Sunday morning, temperatures will warm\nback into the upper 60s to low 70s with a high pressure building\nacross the region. Generally through the remainder of the\nforecast, dry conditions are expected to persist with a warming\ntrend. By the middle of the week, most of the Mid-South will see\nhigh temperatures creep back into the 80s, with some locations\npotentially reaching the mid to upper 80s again by the end of\nnext week.",
